<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">music <a href="/years/1938">1938</a>; words <a href="/years/1943">1943</a>; adopted <a href="/years/1944">1944</a>; revised words adopted <a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Государственный гимн СССР, Gosudarstvenny gimn SSSR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State Anthem of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics was the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the regional anthem of the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic, from 1944 to 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The music was composed by Alexander Alexandrov in 1938 for the Hymn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Bolshevik Party</a>. Its opening bars came from his earlier piece Life Has Become Better, which was based on a remark by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> at the First All-Union Meeting of the Stakhanovites on 17 November 1935. Alexandrov described the piece as a combination of a march with Russian traditional music, particularly the bylina epic songs. Its chord progressions resemble those of Vasily Kalinnikov's overture Bylina, Epic Poem.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> was dissolved in 1943, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> could keep its alliance with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, a replacement was needed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-internationale">The Internationale</a>, which had been the national anthem. A contest in mid to late 1943 drew more than 200 entries, and Stalin personally chose Alexandrov's music, both praising and criticising it. He wanted short words that invoked the coming victory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. His staff called the poets Sergey Mikhalkov and Gabriyel' Arkadyevich Ureklyan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and they completed a first draft overnight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The anthem was first published on 7 November 1943, broadcast on Soviet radio at midnight on 1 January 1944 and officially adopted on 15 March 1944. The words had three stanzas, each followed by a refrain whose second line spoke in turn of friendship, happiness and glory. Stalin and the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were invoked in the second and third verses. Stalin is reported to have opposed putting his name in the words but to have relented when some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> members insisted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After Stalin's death and the process of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a>, the lines about him were considered unacceptable, and from 1956 to 1977 the anthem was performed without words. One exception was at the 1976 Canada Cup ice hockey tournament, where the singer Roger Doucet insisted on singing it. In 1977, to coincide with the 60th anniversary of the October Revolution, new words written in 1970 by Mikhalkov were adopted. The varying refrains gave way to one refrain after all stanzas, and the line praising Stalin and the lines on the Great Patriotic War were dropped. A line about the Soviet flag was replaced by one naming the Communist Party as the party of Lenin.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> in 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> first adopted the Patriotic Song. In December 2000 the federal legislature approved the Soviet anthem's music again as the national anthem of Russia, with new words by Mikhalkov, who had written both earlier versions. The same music was proposed for an anthem of the State Union of Russia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, but that was never adopted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “State Anthem of the Soviet Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alexander Alexandrov, founder of the Red Army's song and dance ensemble and later the composer of the <b>State Anthem of the Soviet Union</b>, set them to music at once.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-sacred-war">The Sacred War</a></li></ul></section>
